  "worked_solution": "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=2$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/a66a57932dbc935b5ed6f74b4f0faa0047c4b7ef58ded46be088dad30fa5eee4.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/52efe97fcffa3da05151b3ffef5305e0c3ec0d567d3f62b964a30740c08a3981.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-2)$ | $(y+1)^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(-2,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-2,-3)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/aa100568c18b31507c775e77ed3201a5aed6f708b541e48046617a43fe9a8304.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
